{"skill":{"slug":"desktop-control-win","displayName":"Desktop Control (Windows)"},"version":{"version":"1.0.1","createdAt":1770841588053,"changelog":"Initial public release. Windows desktop control with window management, input simulation, VS Code helpers, process management, and screen info.","changelogSource":"user","license":null,"files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":14796,"sha256":"917b10e88cf4ac916dddfbe7158ba7b7617c2f7de1512c6731077edc74ee1f4e","contentType":null},{"path":"scripts/app-control.ps1.txt","size":12446,"sha256":"f9a67c2829f220d78a603a5e31c5582482fb131300e186fda8a03771779e9be3","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"scripts/input-sim.ps1.txt","size":8610,"sha256":"2302659be0024baf522affae46ccdc85a84a258cf288c53f1193d23047811a00","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"scripts/process-manager.ps1.txt","size":5807,"sha256":"a7a4e1d232f40a7a04efb9ae226558b91eebe01ba76a3161ec9f9c69ef04346d","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"scripts/screen-info.ps1.txt","size":8723,"sha256":"9a5b5830bff28b9bca51e785923b8f2cf389583e11dfb9b290a23ce124f10b25","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"scripts/vscode-control.ps1.txt","size":10142,"sha256":"fe3b6795bd6b20ce56340103a24b127ad9c123169b90a72ad9c5e4a6b1fbacba","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2308,"sha256":"eff7094165576000c9061f6d48d001283ea8fb97ae676a5ccd9c1b01410b9633","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789061685064,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"b7bfe1cb4b9993d6ce8e1ab103b3e227a58092faff05948d23d1ae40e878f2f5","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/b7bfe1cb4b9993d6ce8e1ab103b3e227a58092faff05948d23d1ae40e878f2f5","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: desktop-control-win\nVersion: 1.0.1\n\nThis skill bundle is classified as suspicious due to its extensive and low-level control over the Windows desktop environment, which, while potentially useful for legitimate automation, exposes significant security risks. The PowerShell scripts allow for arbitrary process execution (`Start-Process` in `app-control.ps1`, `process-manager.ps1`), full keyboard and mouse input simulation (`input-sim.ps1`), reading clipboard content, taking screenshots (`screen-info.ps1`), and installing/uninstalling VSCode extensions (`vscode-control.ps1`). These capabilities, especially clipboard access and screenshot capture, could be leveraged for data exfiltration or unauthorized actions if the AI agent is compromised via prompt injection, despite the 'Safety Rules' in `SKILL.md` attempting to guide responsible use. The explicit instruction in `SKILL.md` to use `powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass` further highlights the elevated risk.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779163284059},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":38,"severity":"MEDIUM","recommendation":"CAUTION","issueCount":3,"checkedAt":1789061685064},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"This skill openly provides Windows desktop control, but its reach is broad enough that users should review it before installing.","dimensions":[{"detail":"Live desktop control is purpose-aligned, but broad interaction with the focused session can affect unrelated applications or expose visible and clipboard data if invoked casually.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The package is publish-only text and instructs users to rename .ps1.txt files into PowerShell scripts; there is no hidden installer, but the documented invocation uses PowerShell ExecutionPolicy Bypass.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The activation wording covers almost any app, window, typing, process, clipboard, or screen task; safety instructions require confirmation for process killing and some window closing, but do not clearly gate screenshot, clipboard reading, arbitrary shortcuts, or mouse clicks.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The scripts do not create services, scheduled tasks, or backdoors, but the VS Code install-extension action force-installs arbitrary extensions, which can persist and later run under the user's account.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The stated purpose matches the shipped scripts, but the capability set includes desktop-wide keyboard and mouse input, window control, process start/kill, screenshots, clipboard access, and VS Code extension changes.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"concern"}],"guidance":"Install only if you intentionally want an agent to control your Windows desktop. Keep it limited to explicit tasks, confirm destructive actions, avoid using screenshot or clipboard commands around sensitive data, and do not let it install VS Code extensions unless you have independently verified the publisher and extension.","findings":"[SQP-1] expected: The broad trigger matches a general desktop-control skill, but it is still a review concern because the skill has high-impact authority across the whole Windows session.\n[SQP-2] expected: Screenshot and clipboard functions are explicitly documented and coherent with the purpose, but the artifact lacks strong privacy warnings or consent guidance before reading sensitive screen or clipboard contents.\n[SQP-2] expected: Keyboard and mouse simulation is central to desktop control, but the script sends input to the current foreground session without built-in target validation or confirmation for sensitive shortcuts and clicks.\n[T08] expected: VS Code extension management is documented, but force-installing an arbitrary extension identifier is a supply-chain and persistence concern without an allowlist, version pinning, or explicit trust step.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789061685064}}}}}